-- Passage rg8-evidence-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4a9f1307-b21b-5181-b921-6c412b472af9', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Shade on Main Street$q$, $q$On a sunny afternoon in July, a dark asphalt parking lot can become far hotter than the air above it. Asphalt absorbs most of the sunlight that strikes it and then releases that energy as heat, often long after sunset. Scientists call a city that stays warmer than the countryside around it an urban heat island.

Trees fight this effect in two ways. First, their leaves block sunlight before it reaches the pavement, so the ground beneath them stays cooler. Second, trees release water vapor through tiny openings in their leaves. When that water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools a person's skin.

In one project, students in the town of Marlow measured surface temperatures along two streets at three o'clock each afternoon for a month. Birch Avenue had a row of large, old oaks, while Foundry Street had almost no trees. The sidewalks on Birch Avenue averaged about twenty degrees cooler than those on Foundry Street. The students also noticed that more people walked on Birch Avenue during the hottest part of the day.

Planting trees is not a quick fix. A young tree may need ten years or more before its branches spread wide enough to shade a sidewalk. Trees also need watering in their first few summers, and their roots can crack pavement if they are planted in spaces that are too small. Even so, many towns now include tree planting in their long-term plans, because the cooling benefits grow larger every year a tree survives.$q$, 'draft')
