-- Passage rg8-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2adc56ed-ca53-5229-88e8-fee9fe7f20ec', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Engineers with Flat Tails$q$, $q$A beaver dam can look like a messy pile of sticks, but it changes the land around it in surprising ways. Beavers build dams across streams by stacking branches, mud, and stones. Water backs up behind the dam and forms a pond. The beavers then build a lodge in the pond, with underwater entrances that keep predators out.

The pond helps far more than the beavers. Slow, deep water gives fish a place to rest and frogs a place to lay eggs. Ducks and herons hunt along the edges. Because the dam slows the stream, mud and soil settle to the bottom instead of washing downstream. Over many years, this soil builds up into rich, wet meadows.

Beaver ponds also store water. In a dry summer, a stream without dams may shrink to a trickle. A stream with several dams often keeps flowing, because water seeps slowly through and around each dam. Some scientists in dry regions have started building simple wooden structures that copy beaver dams, hoping beavers will move in and finish the job.

Beavers can cause problems, too. Their ponds sometimes flood roads, farm fields, or yards. They may cut down trees that people want to keep. In the past, many communities simply removed beavers. Now some use pipes that pass through a dam and keep the pond at a safe level. The beavers stay, and so do the benefits they bring.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
