-- Passage rg8-evidence-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b05202f2-b3cd-56fa-8c59-0cb725edb085', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Turning Lunch Scraps into Soil$q$, $q$Last fall, students at Riverside Middle School noticed how much food was thrown away after lunch. Members of the science club weighed the cafeteria trash for one week. They found that about half of it, by weight, was food scraps such as apple cores, banana peels, and uneaten vegetables.

The club proposed a compost program to the principal, Ms. Adebayo. She agreed to a trial if the club could show it would not create a mess or attract pests. The students set up three bins at the end of each lunch table: one for compost, one for recycling, and one for trash. Club members took turns standing by the bins for the first month to help classmates sort correctly.

The compost went to a fenced area behind the school garden. There, students mixed the scraps with dry leaves and turned the pile with pitchforks every week. Dry leaves add carbon, which balances the nitrogen in food scraps and keeps the pile from smelling. After about four months, the pile had turned into dark, crumbly soil.

By spring, the cafeteria's trash bags had dropped from twelve a day to seven. The custodian, Mr. Lindqvist, said he no longer needed to empty the outdoor dumpster twice a week. The garden club used the finished compost to grow tomatoes and peppers, and some of them ended up in the cafeteria salad bar.

The program still has challenges. On days when no volunteer stands by the bins, more plastic ends up in the compost. The club is now training sixth graders to take over next year.$q$, 'draft')
